{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"activation function activation function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md","fileName":"Activation Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Activation Function","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 00:13\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Artificial Neural Network Structure]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Perceptron","Sigmoid Activation Function","Tanh Activation Function","Rectifier Activation Function","Backpropagation","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"activation function activation function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md","fileName":"Activation Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Activation Function","heading":"Activation Function","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An activation function converts an artificial neuron's weighted input and bias into its output. It determines how strongly the unit activates and gives the network behavior beyond a simple unmodified weighted sum.\n\nA step function produces the binary output used by a [[Perceptron]]. Smooth alternatives include the [[Sigmoid Activation Function]] and [[Tanh Activation Function]], while the [[Rectifier Activation Function]] suppresses negative input and passes positive input proportionally.\n\nKelleher explains why the nonlinear step matters: composing layers that only calculate weighted sums still gives an overall linear mapping, so such a network cannot represent a genuinely nonlinear input-to-output relationship. A nonlinear activation changes that capacity. The choice also affects training: ordinary [[Backpropagation]] needs usable derivatives, which a discontinuous threshold does not supply; saturated sigmoid regions can make those derivatives very small.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Perceptron","Sigmoid Activation Function","Tanh Activation Function","Rectifier Activation Function","Backpropagation","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"activation function activation function","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Activation Function.md","fileName":"Activation Function.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Activation Function","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[algorithms.epub]]\n\n[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Artificial Neural Network Structure","Perceptron","Sigmoid Activation Function","Tanh Activation Function","Rectifier Activation Function","Backpropagation","algorithms.epub","deeplearning_mit.epub"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}